No existing audit infrastructure or migrations directory — schema presumably lives elsewhere. Here's how I'd build it, given what's here.

**Recommendation: DB-level triggers, not application-level logging.** `db.js` already funnels every write through one `write()` function, which makes app-level logging tempting — but that only guarantees coverage as long as every future write goes through that helper. Compliance's "for every write" is closer to a Postgres trigger on each audited table: it fires on any `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE` regardless of code path (a route someone adds later, a one-off admin script, a migration), which app-level logging can't promise.

The piece that doesn't fall out for free with triggers is **who** — a trigger has no idea which HTTP user issued the write. Fix: have `write()` set a transaction-local actor before the write, inside the transaction it already opens:

```js
async function write(text, params, actorId) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.actor_id', $1, true)", [actorId]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Callers pass `req.user.id` (already resolved by `sessionUser` in `auth.js`). One line changed at every call site (`routes/accounts.js`, `routes/orders.js`) instead of audit logic in each handler.

Then, per audited table:

```sql
create table audit_log (
  id bigserial primary key,
  table_name text not null,
  row_id text not null,
  action text not null,          -- insert/update/delete
  old_row jsonb,
  new_row jsonb,
  actor_id text,
  changed_at timestamptz not null default now()
);
revoke update, delete on audit_log from app_role;   -- append-only, even for the app itself

create function audit_row() returns trigger as $$
begin
  insert into audit_log(table_name, row_id, action, old_row, new_row, actor_id)
  values (TG_TABLE_NAME, coalesce(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text, lower(TG_OP),
          case when TG_OP <> 'INSERT' then to_jsonb(OLD) end,
          case when TG_OP <> 'DELETE' then to_jsonb(NEW) end,
          current_setting('app.actor_id', true));
  return coalesce(NEW, OLD);
end;
$$ language plpgsql;

create trigger accounts_audit after insert or update or delete on accounts
  for each row execute function audit_row();
create trigger orders_audit after insert or update or delete on orders
  for each row execute function audit_row();
```

